## Solution

We solve the IVP
$$y'' + y' + xy = 0,\qquad y(0)=y'(0)=1.$$

### Step 1. Remove the first-derivative term

Set $y(x)=e^{-x/2}\,u(x)$. Then
$$y'=e^{-x/2}\!\left(u'-\tfrac12 u\right),\qquad y''=e^{-x/2}\!\left(u''-u'+\tfrac14 u\right).$$
Substituting,
$$y''+y'+xy=e^{-x/2}\Big[u''-u'+\tfrac14 u+u'-\tfrac12 u+xu\Big]=e^{-x/2}\Big[u''+\Big(x-\tfrac14\Big)u\Big].$$
Hence $u$ satisfies
$$u''+\Big(x-\tfrac14\Big)u=0.\tag{1}$$

### Step 2. Reduce to the Airy equation

Let $s=x-\tfrac14$ (so $ds/dx=1$). Equation (1) becomes
$$\frac{d^2u}{ds^2}+s\,u=0,\qquad\text{i.e.}\quad u''-(-s)u=0,$$
the standard Airy equation in the variable $z=-s=\tfrac14-x$. Its general solution is
$$u(x)=c_1\,\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)+c_2\,\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right),$$
and therefore
$$y(x)=e^{-x/2}\Big[c_1\,\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)+c_2\,\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)\Big].\tag{2}$$

### Step 3. Apply the initial conditions

From $y(0)=1$:
$$c_1\,\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+c_2\,\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=1.\tag{3}$$

Since $y'=e^{-x/2}\!\left(u'-\tfrac12 u\right)$ and $u'(x)=-c_1\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)-c_2\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)$, the condition $y'(0)=1$ gives
$$u'(0)-\tfrac12 u(0)=1\;\Longrightarrow\;-c_1\,\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)-c_2\,\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)-\tfrac12=1,$$
i.e.
$$c_1\,\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+c_2\,\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=-\tfrac32.\tag{4}$$

### Step 4. Solve the linear system

Using the Airy Wronskian $\mathrm{Ai}(z)\mathrm{Bi}'(z)-\mathrm{Ai}'(z)\mathrm{Bi}(z)=\dfrac1\pi$, Cramer's rule on (3),(4) yields
$$c_1=\pi\!\left[\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+\tfrac32\,\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right],\qquad
c_2=-\pi\!\left[\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+\tfrac32\,\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right].$$

### Step 5. Final answer

Substituting $c_1,c_2$ into (2):

$$\boxed{\,y(x)=\pi\,e^{-x/2}\!\left[\left(\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+\tfrac32\,\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right)\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)-\left(\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+\tfrac32\,\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right)\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14-x\right)\right]\,}.$$

**Verification of $y(0)=1$:** at $x=0$ the bracket becomes
$$\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\mathrm{Bi}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)+\tfrac32\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)-\tfrac32\mathrm{Ai}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)-\mathrm{Ai}'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\mathrm{Bi}\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=\frac1\pi,$$
so $y(0)=\pi\cdot(1/\pi)=1$. ✓

**Power-series check.** With $y=\sum a_nx^n$, $a_0=a_1=1$, the recurrence
$$a_{n+2}=-\frac{(n+1)a_{n+1}+a_{n-1}}{(n+2)(n+1)}\quad(n\ge1),\qquad 2a_2+a_1=0,$$
gives $a_2=-\tfrac12,\;a_3=0,\;a_4=-\tfrac1{12},\;a_5=\tfrac1{24},\;a_6=-\tfrac1{144},\ldots$, which is the Taylor expansion of the closed form above about $x=0$.

